<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belarus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belarus is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>. Its lands belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> and then for centuries to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, before passing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in the partitions of Poland. After serving as the Byelorussian Soviet Republic it became independent in 1991, and it has been ruled by Alexander Lukashenko since 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belarus.jpg" alt="Belarus" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belarus.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Belarus is a landlocked country of 207,600 square kilometres, lying between latitudes 51° and 57° N and measuring 560 kilometres from north to south and 650 from west to east. It is relatively flat, with large tracts of marshy land, about 40% forest, and 11,000 lakes.</p><p>Three major rivers run through it: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> flows west toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, the Pripyat flows east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, and the Dnieper flows south toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. The highest point is Dzyarzhynskaya Hara, at 345 metres. About 70% of the radiation from the 1986 Chernobyl nuclear disaster in neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> entered Belarusian territory, and about a fifth of the land was affected by fallout.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Baltic tribes first permanently settled the region in the 3rd century, and around the 5th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> took it over, and the gradual assimilation of the Baltic settlers into Slavic culture was peaceful. In the 9th century it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, a vast East Slavic state ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurikids</a>, which split into independent principalities on the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> in 1054.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-nemiga-river">Battle on the Nemiga River</a> in 1067 is considered the founding date of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>. The lands avoided the brunt of the 13th-century Mongol invasion and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>; the annals affirm an alliance and united foreign policy between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> for decades. The duchy fought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> in 1410.</p><p>By the 14th century the lands had come fully under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, a period lasting 500 years until the partitions of 1792–1795 placed Belarus within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> for the first time. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byelorussian-ssr">Byelorussian SSR</a>, formed after the Russian Revolution, became a founding republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1922. After the Polish-Soviet War (1918–1921) Belarus lost almost half its territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it lost about a quarter of its population and half of its economic resources. Many prominent Belarusian intellectuals were exiled or killed in Stalinist purges in the 1930s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Belarus proclaimed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> on 27 July 1990 and gained independence on 25 August 1991. Belarus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> signed a treaty in 2000 forming the Union State. The official languages are Russian and Belarusian. Francysk Skaryna's Bible, published between 1517 and 1525, was the first book printed in Belarus or anywhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Belarus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom's industry made Warsaw and Łódź major cities, and its territory corresponds roughly to the Lublin, Łódź, Masovian, Podlaskie and Holy Cross Voivodeships of Poland and parts of Lithuania and <b>Belarus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In April 1999 the Federal Assembly voted to join the Union State of Russia and <b>Belarus</b>, a decision never acted on.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Grand Duchy of Lithuania grew from a pagan Baltic realm into one of the largest states of medieval Europe, ruling most of present-day <b>Belarus</b> and Ukraine, and through its union with Poland it shaped the religious, legal and national history of eastern Europe for more than five centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Ukraine, <b>Belarus</b> and Russia all regard Rus' as the foundation of their history, and the question of its legacy, central to the national narratives of all three, remains politically charged today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On 8 December 1991 Yeltsin and the leaders of Ukraine and <b>Belarus</b> signed the Belovezha Accords, declaring that the Soviet Union had ceased to exist and founding the Commonwealth of Independent States.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Civil War of 1918–1921 the Red Army, organised by Leon Trotsky, defeated the White armies and foreign intervention forces, and Soviet republics were established or restored by force in Ukraine, <b>Belarus</b> and the Transcaucasus, while Finland, Poland and the Baltic states secured their independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
